To change the font of the `<h1>` element, you can use the `font-family` property in CSS. Here's an example:

```css
h1 {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

In this code:

- `h1` is the selector, which targets all `<h1>` elements on the page.
- `font-family` is the property, which specifies the font family to be used.
- `Arial, sans-serif` is the value, which is a list of font families. The first font family specified (in this case, `Arial`) is the primary font, and the second font family (`sans-serif`) is the fallback font in case the primary font is not available.

You can replace `Arial` with any other font family you prefer, such as `Times New Roman`, `Helvetica`, `Georgia`, or a custom font.

If you want to apply this style to a specific `<h1>` element, you can use an ID or class selector instead of the universal `h1` selector:

```css
#headline {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Or:

```css
.headline {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Then, in your HTML, you would add the ID or class to the `<h1>` element:

```html
<h1 id="headline">This is a headline</h1>
```

Or:

```html
<h1 class="headline">This is a headline</h1>
```

You can also use a more modern approach with CSS variables (also known as custom properties) to make it easier to manage and change the font family across your project:

```css
:root {
  --font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

h1 {
  font-family: var(--font-family);
}
```

This way, you can easily change the font family by updating the value of the `--font-family` variable in one place.

